    {
      "id": "HCM-01",
      "chapter": "global",
      "anchor": "global-higher-corank-theorem",
      "title": "A weighted primitive mean controls the middle coranks",
      "statement": "For 2≤s≤k−2 and j=k−s, the normalized mean of primitive rank-j sublattices with dual quotient of order d² is at most (d/t)^k(dt)^(Cs+C log f) MₛMⱼ/Mₖ.",
      "hypotheses": "The integral complement of a common-frame row over the finite-unramified CM extension, totally definite of rank k, with 𝒪_E/(T) as its discriminant module and a completely split prime T≥3. Here t=T^f, d≥1, b≥1, m≥2, 0<c₀≤c_l, log rd(E)≤(log b)/c_l, C is any real ≥max(16,1/c₀), and H≥b^(Cm²f).",
      "correspondence": "The subspace covering, the two fixed-discriminant mass bounds and the gluing bound give an unnormalized mass of at most MⱼMₛ d^j(dt)^(9s+8+4log₂(2f)). Divide by the split-prime lower bound on the genus mass.",
