    {
      "id": "HCM-02",
      "chapter": "global",
      "anchor": "global-higher-small-ranks",
      "title": "Ranks one and zero use a direct count in each lattice",
      "statement": "For 2≤s≤k with k−s equal to 0 or 1, the genus mean of the number of primitive rank-(k−s) intersections of dual-quotient order d² is at most (d/t)^k (dt)^(Cs+C log f) (Ck)^(kf) (Ht)^(Ck).",
      "hypotheses": "The integral complement of a common-frame row over the finite-unramified CM extension, totally definite of rank k, with 𝒪_E/(T) as its discriminant module and a completely split prime T≥3. Here t=T^f, d≥1, b≥1, m≥2, 0<c₀≤c_l, log rd(E)≤(log b)/c_l, C is any real ≥max(16,1/c₀), and H≥b^(Cm²f).",
      "correspondence": "An individual vector count bounds lines by (9 rd(E))^(kf)d^k. The zero space contributes at most once. Taking the weighted mean and using the height inequality supplies the endpoint factors.",
