    {
      "id": "HCM-03",
      "chapter": "global",
      "anchor": "global-higher-pair-use",
      "title": "Only one primitive genus mean occurs in the ordered-pair count",
      "statement": "For fixed relevant labels and allowed determinant cutoff Δ, the pair mean is bounded by K² times the sum of the exact primitive means multiplied by (Δ/d)^(k+log₂(2f)+1). Each summand has the same factor (Δ/t)^k.",
      "hypotheses": "The integral complement of a common-frame row over the finite-unramified CM extension, totally definite of rank k, with 𝒪_E/(T) as its discriminant module and a completely split prime T≥3. Here t=T^f, d≥1, b≥1, m≥2, 0<c₀≤c_l, log rd(E)≤(log b)/c_l, C is any real ≥max(16,1/c₀), and H≥b^(Cm²f). Both flags have the same witnessing lattice, with per-flag allowance K. The actual frame estimate assumes the determinant cutoff is within its label height allowance.",
      "correspondence": "The pair count uses the common witness and two independent choices of flags above it. Isometry invariance transports the primitive count across row and genus representatives before the finite determinant sum is bounded.",
